WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:06.950
Imagine you’re doing your daily swiping
left on whatever app you kids are using these

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:15.090
days, every guy you come across is whatever,
but you come across this knee-buckling bio:

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:21.090
All-around Manly Man. Consummate outdoorsman,
marlin fisherman, bullfighter, big game hunter.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:27.410
War hero. German submarine hunter. Bestselling
novelist. King of the Short Story. Nobel and

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.539
Pulitzer prize winner. Forever changed the
World of Literature.&nbsp;

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:32.900
Hmm. interesting.&nbsp;

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.860
So, ok, here’s the problem with tackling
Ernest Hemingway—Ernest Hemingway himself.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.150
While the iconic author is mostly known for
his feats of literary prowess, from The Sun

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:48.390
Also Rises to For Whom the Bell Tolls, to
countless short stories—perhaps his greatest

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.550
fiction of all is his own self-mythologizing.
As his brand grew in the 1920’s and 30’s,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.180
so too grew his celebrity and, well, his ego.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.360
So, with Ernie all the while throwing so much
self-mythologizing in the mix that it became

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.601
nearly impossible to separate the Man from
the Myth.

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:10.499
But gosh darn it, we’re going to try.

00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:13.710
So if you’ve ever taken a writing course,
you’re probably familiar with Hemingway’s

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.619
iceberg Theory—that the words on the page
are like part of the iceberg that’s above

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:22.960
the water and what is OMITTED or not said
is the much more substantive part. Basically,

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.820
it’s a “keep simple, stupid” methodology
prioritizing qualities like: (display points

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.439
as visuals, not said aloud). All of this done
in the pursuit of writing what Hemingway described

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:34.439
as “one true sentence”.&nbsp;

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:39.049
But the greatest irony is that as Hemmingway
transitioned from journalism into fiction—short

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:43.329
stories at first and then novels—his relationship
with the truth and the facts, so cut-and-dry

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:47.850
from his years as a reporter, started to get
messy. Said Hemmingway:&nbsp;

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.470
“Writers of fiction are only super-liars
who if they know enough and are disciplined

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:57.840
can make their lies truer than the truth….
That is all a writer of fiction is.”

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.179
Life experiences were everything to Hemmingway,
for he believed his “lies,” his fiction,

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:06.200
could only be as good as the experiences from
which they were mined. It’s no wonder then

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:10.830
that Hemmingway had some pretty wild adventures,
including:

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.420
Getting into two back-to-back plane crashes!
fighting with Orson Welles after calling him

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.640
an “effeminate boy of the theatre”!
shooting himself in both legs while trying

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.470
to shoot a shark!
trying to kill himself by walking straight

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.690
toward a plane propeller!
saving a friend from getting gored to death

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.600
by an enraged bull!
getting married four times!

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.610
being a spy against the Germans!
hanging out with movie stars like Ingrid Bergman

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.290
and Gary Cooper!
and more

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.990
Given that Hemmingway’s life experiences
were such essential fodder for his stories,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:41.650
it’s no wonder he was very protective of
them. After the publication of a 1949 biographical

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:46.190
profile in Life magazine, he was worried that
the stories covered in the piece “used up”

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:50.260
those life experiences, making it impossible
for him to mine them in his later writings:

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.760
“[The Life profile] made me feel bad to
lose things I was happy about because nobody

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.760
knew them.”&nbsp;

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.030
Born to Ed and Grace Hemingway in 1899, Hemmingway
had the kind of hands-on-outdoorsy uprearing

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.670
one would with only the scantiest knowledge
of the author could imagine: From his mother

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.900
claiming he could shoot and load a gun from
the age of three, to his father’s endless

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.750
polemics on the ethics of hunting and fishing,
to his grandpa lovingly dubbing him “The

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:18.000
Great Hunter Ernest MIller”, it seemed his
own family was gassing him up to be a protagonist

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.490
in one of his books.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:23.390
Shortly after graduating high school, Hemingway
worked as a reporter for the Kansas Star,

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:28.030
where he began honing his “one true sentences”,
and shortly after volunteered to become an

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.540
ambulance driver in Italy during World War
One. And here, an almost superhero-like narrative

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.950
arises -- one that claims Hemmingway won a
medal of valor for carrying multiple soldiers

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:41.400
to safety, despite his legs being torn apart
by enemy shrapnel. And as cool as it sounds

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.760
on paper, the validity of said narrative raised
more than a few eyebrows. Observed Hemingway

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.820
biographer Mary V Dearborn:

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.870
Many versions of the story would surface in
the weeks and months to come as he told, retold,

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.620
and embellished the tale of his exploits.
Indeed, he would return to his wounding and

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.040
his heroism obsessively, for the rest of his
life, changing his report as the occasion

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.040
demanded.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.290
Regardless of what truly happened, Hemmingway’s
bravery and heroism cannot be denied, but

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:08.730
Hemmingway’s constant revisionist history
makes it frustrating to give credit where

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:13.130
credit is due. For Hemmingway, though, this
muddling of fact and fiction would continue

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:18.289
to benefit him and his efforts to build upon
the Hemingway Myth long after his heroic war

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:23.080
story. And boy let me tell you, there would
be some real whoppers to come.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:27.289
Hemmingway’s time in Europe, particularly
Paris and Spain, that is the stuff of legend.

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:31.930
So much of the Hemingway Myth is born here,
including Hemmingway’s romantic view of

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:35.789
bullfighting, writers like James Joyce and
Gertrude Stein and F. Scott Fitzgerald toiling

00:04:35.789 --> 00:04:41.569
away on their masterpieces in Parisian cafés,
and most especially, the concept of the “Lost

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:46.580
Generation,” a term Gertrude Stein used
to describe young, aimless American expatriates

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.150
post-World War I.&nbsp;

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.990
The idea of the Lost Generation was forever
memorialized in Hemmingway’s first major

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:57.590
novel, The Sun Also Rises, which many consider
to be his masterpiece. And which he wrote

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:05.710
in six weeks. Sigh… if only… When Rises
was published, aside from catapulting Hemmingway

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:10.410
to super-writerdom, it also began a trend
of Hemmingway using his work to take shots

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:15.129
at people he knew, oftentimes friends and
acquaintances who had no idea Hemmingway was

00:05:15.129 --> 00:05:16.509
even pissed at them.

00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:21.330
The first to be blindsided was Harold Loeb,
a friend who helped Hemmingway get his start

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:26.289
by fighting for the publication of In Our
Time. Imagine Loeb’s surprise when he appeared

00:05:26.289 --> 00:05:30.990
as the villain in The Sun Also Rises. Sure,
his name was changed to Robert Cohn, along

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:37.419
with a few personal traits, but it was unmistakably
him. Nobody knows exactly why Hemmingway did

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:43.180
this to Loeb, and this confusion is a common
thread with Hemmingway’s fiction-as-revenge.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:48.449
Donald Ogden Stewart, a former friend of Hemmingway’s
(Hemmingway would come to have a lot of former

00:05:48.449 --> 00:05:49.449
friends), observed:&nbsp;

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:53.750
…if Hem had been just plain mean you wouldn’t
have noticed it. But he wasn’t mean, he

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:56.780
was charismatic; and it was for this very
reason that the mean streak startled you so

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.360
when it came to the surface.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.319
Examples of Hemmingway weaponizing his writing
are so numerous, you’d be hard-pressed to

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:06.259
find one of his works that didn’t have at
least one diss track. But perhaps the most

00:06:06.259 --> 00:06:13.490
tragic is his relentless abuse of F. Scott
Fitzgerald, whose life was also already A

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:17.629
Hot Mess (™). As with Harold Loeb, Scott
and Hemmingway were friends, but through the

00:06:17.629 --> 00:06:23.490
years it was as if Hemmingway had some inexorable
drive to sabotage their friendship. When “The

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:28.080
Snows of Kilimanjaro” first appeared in
Esquire, Hemmingway used Scott’s real name,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.759
to which Scott responded, “Please lay off
me in print.” And this doesn’t even include

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:37.759
some of the smack talk in A Moveable Feast,
which I can’t repeat here out of concern

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:40.310
for Viewers Like You.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.889
Just before Scott’s death, Hemmingway wrote
to a friend, backhand-complimenting Scott

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.460
as only Hemmingway could: “I always had
a very stupid little boy&nbsp;feeling of superiority

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:54.250
about Scott—like a tough little boy sneering
at a delicate but talented little boy.”

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:59.069
With Hemmingway himself so inextricably linked
to his work, to praise one was to praise the

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:04.479
other, in so doing feeding the ever-hungry
Hemingway Ego Machine. Likewise, an assault

00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:09.569
on one was an assault on the other. Hemmingway
could certainly dish it out, but he absolutely

00:07:09.569 --> 00:07:10.569
could not take it.&nbsp;

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:14.800
As Hemmingway’s notoriety reached boundless
heights, he basically painted a target on

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.509
his own back. After the great success of A
Farewell to Arms, another masterful novel

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:24.539
published three years after The Sun Also Rises,
Hemingway biographer Mary V. Dearborn notes:

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:29.069
The critical vultures were definitely circling…
the attacks on him took a more personal tone,

00:07:29.069 --> 00:07:33.830
perhaps, than did criticism about other writers.
Hemingway’s work conveyed not just stories

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:38.339
“in our time,” but, implicitly, his own
attitudes, personality, and values.&nbsp;

00:07:38.339 --> 00:07:42.180
As Hemmingway the Man became more and more
intertwined with his characters, the idea

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:47.250
of the Hemingway hero emerged. The Hemingway
hero (almost always a man) is a person of

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.720
profoundly rooted principles like courage,
honor, and dogged determinism. These morals

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.159
are unshakeable in the face of any obstacle,
no matter how formidable.&nbsp;

00:07:57.159 --> 00:08:01.139
Critic Philip Young entered the Hemingway
Octagon with his “wound” theory of Hemmingway

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:05.379
and his protagonists. As one of many critics
who viewed the Hemingway hero as a stand-in

00:08:05.379 --> 00:08:09.949
for Hemmingway himself, Young proffered that
the Hemingway hero strictly adhered to his

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:14.059
codes as a sort of compensation for his battle
wounds. Says Young:

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:18.659
The Hemingway hero, the big, tough, outdoor
man, is also the wounded man, and descriptions

00:08:18.659 --> 00:08:23.080
of certain scenes in the life of Nick Adams
have explained how he got that way. The man

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:28.039
will die a thousand times before his death,
but from his wounds he would never recover

00:08:28.039 --> 00:08:31.409
so long as Hemingway lived and recorded his
adventures

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:37.180
Other critics were not so … precious…
with Hemingway’s mold of a hero, with critics

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:43.550
like Wyndham Lewis referring to them as “loutish,
oafish marionettes” and Gertrude Stein wannabes.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:47.389
Fellow writer Max Eastman straight up kicked
the hornet’s nest by thoroughly dunking

00:08:47.389 --> 00:08:51.870
on Hemingway’s bullfighting memoir Death
in the Afternoon, disgusted with how he saw

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:56.930
it as romanticizing animal cruelty in the
name of Hemingway’s macho image and calling

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:02.160
it “a literary style, you might say, of
wearing false hair on the chest.” This allegedly

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.839
culminated in Hemmingway and Eastman going
chest hair to chest hair when they ran into

00:09:06.839 --> 00:09:12.139
each other and the author allegedly ripped
open both of their shirts to see who had more

00:09:12.139 --> 00:09:17.620
chest hair and subsequently telling the press
that “[Eastman]&nbsp; jumped at me like a woman—clawing,

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:23.970
you know, with his open hands.”&nbsp;Hey, you
can’t have a fight without some casual misogyny

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.579
Hemmingway’s lasting success in mythologizing
himself is no more evident than in his reputation

00:09:28.579 --> 00:09:34.720
as the Apotheosis of Masculinity. His refusal
to regularly bathe, all his fightin’ and

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.410
huntin’ and boxin’ and drinkin’. Heck,
Hemmingway even claimed his novel For Whom

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:45.100
the Bell Tolls was so sexy that his ex-Marine
pal Ben Finney got hot and bothered by it.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:47.019
Which ew, TMI Ernie.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:50.699
But with the passage of time, Hemingway’s
aging forced him to confront some hard truths

00:09:50.699 --> 00:09:55.870
about himself—getting balder and heavier
resulted in women seeing him less as the handsome

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:01.509
lothario he once fancied himself and more
as a father figure, the white-bearded “Papa”

00:10:01.509 --> 00:10:05.750
Hemingway.&nbsp; But so, so much worse was his
declining mental health. The manic depression

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:10.339
that ran in his family was continuing to rear
its ugly head as Hemmingway got older, plunging

00:10:10.339 --> 00:10:15.630
him into wave after wave of mania and depression,
all the while fueled by his worsening alcoholism.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:19.910
This created a volatile cocktail that was
nearly impossible for doctors to adequately

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.769
diagnose, especially at a time when we were
only beginning to understand the complexities

00:10:23.769 --> 00:10:29.890
of mental health. So, they fed him pills.
Lots of pills. Just after receiving the Nobel

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:34.690
Prize in 1954, Hemmingway told Time magazine,
“I have to take so many pills, they have

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:37.399
to fight among themselves if I take them too
close together.”

00:10:37.399 --> 00:10:40.680
For those who had been witnessing Hemmingway’s
downward spiral throughout the final years

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:45.470
of his life, they seemed to know deep down
what they could not admit out loud—that

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:50.709
Hemmingway was on the same tragic path as
his father once was. Like his father before

00:10:50.709 --> 00:10:56.540
him, Ernest Hemingway took his own life the
morning of July 2, 1961 at the age of 61.&nbsp;

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:00.290
Several years after Hemmingway’s death,
a memoir about his time in Paris, A Moveable

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:05.319
Feast, would be published, and the original
1964 edition would begin with an odd statement,

00:11:05.319 --> 00:11:06.649
especially for a memoir:&nbsp;

00:11:06.649 --> 00:11:10.670
“If the reader prefers, this book may be
regarded as fiction. But there is always the

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:15.550
chance that such a book of fiction may throw
some light on what has been written as fact.”

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:21.079
Fiction? A memoir? It was as if Hemmingway
was winking at his readers from beyond the

00:11:21.079 --> 00:11:25.529
grave—even a supposed real-life account
of his time in Paris should be read with about

00:11:25.529 --> 00:11:27.899
the same standard of facts as The Sun Also
Rises.

00:11:27.899 --> 00:11:32.620
But it was never facts Hemmingway was interested
in. He was after larger truths, the deeper

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:37.870
truths of what it is to be human. Facts were
just the bothersome details that got in the

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:38.870
way.&nbsp;

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:42.750
A Moveable Feast contains another line that
is among Hemmingway’s most oft-quoted: “They

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:46.630
say the seeds of what we will do are in all
of us, but it always seemed to me that in

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:51.129
those who make jokes in life the &nbsp; &nbsp; seeds
are covered with better soil and with a higher

00:11:51.129 --> 00:11:52.709
grade of manure.”&nbsp;

00:11:52.709 --> 00:11:56.441
Despite Hemmingway’s life-long contentious
battle with the facts, he was always true

00:11:56.441 --> 00:12:02.180
to himself. As a fiction writer, he was by
necessity an admitted “super-liar.” A

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:06.009
super-liar forever tilling the garden of his
legacy with the highest grade of manure there

00:12:06.009 --> 00:12:09.940
is -- Ernest Hemingway’s Grade-A Premium
Bullshit-- BS

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:14.570
One more thing — you should check out the
new Hemingway documentary from Ken Burns and

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:21.740
Lynn Novick on PBS. The three-part doc goes
live on Monday, April 5 at 8/7 central, and

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:27.730
you can stream it on PBS.org or the PBS Video
app on your Apple TV, Roku, Chromecast and

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:31.930
other fancy tv boxes. Check the links in the
description for more!

